# ☕ Java — Day 03 Notes

**Source:** Coder Army | Aditya Tandon Sir
**Topic:** Variables, Data Types, Comments, Literals

---

## Pehle Ye Socho — Data Store Kahan Hota Hai?

Jab bhi hum program mein kuch value use karte hain — jaise kisi ka age, name, marks — toh wo **memory mein store** hoti hai।

Par memory ko kaise batayenge ki **kitni jagah chahiye** aur **kis type ka data** store hoga?

Yehi kaam karta hai — **Variable + Data Type** ka combination! 🎯

---

## Variable Kya Hai?

Variable = **memory ka ek named box** jisme data store hota hai।

```java
int age = 20;
```

Iska matlab:
- `int` → box ka type (integer number store hoga)
- `age` → box ka naam
- `20` → box mein rakha data

**Kyun zaroori hai naam?**
Taaki baad mein us data ko **naam se access** kar sako — memory address yaad nahi karna padta!

---

## Comment Kya Hota Hai?

Comment = **code ke andar explanation** — jo Java compiler ignore karta hai।

```java
// Ye single line comment hai

/* Ye
   multi-line
   comment hai */
```

**Kyun use karte hain?**
- Code samjhane ke liye — apne future self ke liye bhi! 😄
- Compiler inhe skip karta hai — program par koi effect nahi

---

## Literal Kya Hota Hai?

Literal = **directly likhi hui fixed value** — jo variable mein store hoti hai।

```java
int age = 20;      // 20 ek literal hai
float pi = 3.14f;  // 3.14f ek literal hai
char c = 'A';      // 'A' ek literal hai
```

Simple shabdon mein — **jo value tune seedha likhi, wo literal hai!**

---

## Data Types — 4 Main Types

Java mein data 4 tarah ka hota hai:

```
Data Types
├── int     → whole numbers (1, 2, 100, -50)
├── float   → decimal numbers (3.14, 9.8)
├── char    → single character ('A', 'z', '9')
└── boolean → true ya false sirf
```

Ab inhe **depth mein** samjhte hain 👇

---

## 1. Integer Types — 4 Types

Poore numbers store karne ke liye — but **size alag alag** hota hai:

| Type | Size | Range | Kab use karein |
|------|------|-------|----------------|
| **byte** | 1 byte | -128 to 127 | Bahut chhota number |
| **short** | 2 bytes | -32,768 to 32,767 | Medium chhota |
| **int** | 4 bytes | -2 billion to 2 billion | Default — mostly yehi use hota hai |
| **long** | 8 bytes | Bahut bada number | Jab int se bada chahiye |

```java
byte b = 100;
short s = 1000;
int age = 20;         // sabse common
long population = 8000000000L;  // L lagana zaroori!
```

**Kyun itne types?**
Memory bachane ke liye! Agar chhota number store karna hai toh `int` kyun use karein — `byte` se kaam chalao। 💡

---

## 2. Float Types — 2 Types

Decimal numbers ke liye:

| Type | Size | Precision | Kab use karein |
|------|------|-----------|----------------|
| **float** | 4 bytes | Single precision (~7 digits) | Jab exact precision nahi chahiye |
| **double** | 8 bytes | Double precision (~15 digits) | Jab zyada accuracy chahiye |

```java
float pi = 3.14f;        // f lagana zaroori!
double salary = 99999.99; // default decimal type
```

**Single vs Double Precision — Kya Matlab?**
- `float` = **7 decimal digits** tak accurate
- `double` = **15 decimal digits** tak accurate

Socho — scientific calculations mein agar 0.000001 ka bhi farak matter karta hai — toh `double` use karo!

> **Default rule:** Java mein decimal number likhne par automatically `double` maana jaata hai — isliye float mein `f` lagana padta hai!

---

## 3. Char — C/C++ Se Alag Kyun?

```java
char c = 'A';
```

| | C/C++ | Java |
|--|-------|------|
| Size | 1 byte | **2 bytes** |
| Encoding | ASCII | **Unicode (UTF-16)** |

**Kyun Java ne 2 bytes rakha?**

C/C++ mein `char` sirf **ASCII** support karta tha — matlab sirf English characters (A-Z, 0-9 etc.) — 128 characters total।

But Java **international language** ke liye bana tha — Hindi, Chinese, Arabic, Japanese — sab support karna tha।

Isliye Java ne **Unicode** adopt kiya — jisme **65,536 characters** fit hote hain! Aur itne characters ke liye **2 bytes** chahiye।

```java
char hindi = 'अ';   // Java mein possible hai!
char c = 'A';
```

> **Key insight:** Ye Java ki global thinking hai — sirf English nahi, poori duniya ke characters support karo! 🌍

---

## 4. Boolean

```java
boolean isPass = true;
boolean isRaining = false;
```

- Sirf **2 values** — `true` ya `false`
- Conditions aur decisions ke liye use hota hai
- Size technically 1 bit — but JVM internally 1 byte use karta hai

---

## Special Literals — Octal aur Hexadecimal

Ye ek **interesting cheez** hai — numbers sirf decimal mein nahi likhe jaate!

### Octal (Base 8) — `0` se shuru karo

```java
int octal = 0123;  // pehle 0 lagao
```

- `0` prefix = Java samjhega ye **octal number** hai
- `0123` octal = `83` decimal

**Kab use hota hai?** File permissions (Linux), low-level programming mein।

### Hexadecimal (Base 16) — `0X` se shuru karo

```java
int hex = 0X1F;   // pehle 0X lagao
```

- `0X` prefix = Java samjhega ye **hexadecimal** hai
- `0X1F` = `31` decimal
- Digits: 0-9 aur A-F (A=10, B=11... F=15)

**Kab use hota hai?** Colors (RGB codes), memory addresses, networking mein।

```
Normal:  int a = 255;     // decimal
Octal:   int b = 0377;    // 0 se shuru
Hexa:    int c = 0XFF;    // 0X se shuru
// teeno same value store karte hain — 255!
```

---

## Poora Picture Ek Saath

```
Java Data Types
│
├── int (4 types)
│   ├── byte  → 1 byte
│   ├── short → 2 bytes
│   ├── int   → 4 bytes ← most common
│   └── long  → 8 bytes
│
├── float (2 types)
│   ├── float  → 4 bytes (single precision)
│   └── double → 8 bytes (double precision) ← more accurate
│
├── char
│   └── 2 bytes ← Unicode (C/C++ se alag!)
│
└── boolean
    └── true / false only
```

